The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ff = fuse_file_alloc(fm, open);
```
```c
kfree(ff->args);
ff->args = NULL;
```
Reason:
When `fc->no_open` is set, `open` is false, so `fuse_file_alloc()` does not allocate `ff->args->release_args`. Consequently, `fuse_prepare_release()` skips taking an inode reference (since `ff->args` is NULL). This allows the inode to be evicted while a readahead folio is locked. If the server triggers memory reclaim during readahead, reclaim tries to evict the inode, which calls `truncate_inode_pages_range()` and blocks waiting for the locked folio. Meanwhile, readahead cannot release the lock because it is blocked in reclaim, causing a deadlock. The unconditional freeing of `ff->args` on `ENOSYS` (even for non-directories) removes the only reference holder, further enabling premature eviction.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bool release = !isdir || open;
```
```c
ff = fuse_file_alloc(fm, release);
```
```c
if (isdir) {
    /* No release needed */
    kfree(ff->args);
    ff->args = NULL;
    fc->no_opendir = 1;
} else {
    fc->no_open = 1;
}
```
Reason:
By setting `release = !isdir || open`, the `fuse_file_alloc()` now always allocates `ff->args->release_args` for regular files (`!isdir` is true), even when the server does not implement `open`. This ensures `fuse_prepare_release()` will always take an inode reference, preventing premature eviction. For directories, `release` remains false when `opendir` is not implemented, so the old behavior is preserved. Additionally, the `ENOSYS` error path only frees `ff->args` for directories, while for regular files the allocated args are kept to hold the inode reference. This eliminates the deadlock because the inode reference is held until the file is fully released, blocking eviction during inflight operations like readahead.

